==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|gem-pro|īsarnan|lang=ang}}, probably from {{proto|cel-pro|īsarno-|lang=ang}}.  Cognates include Old Saxon {{term|isarn|īsarn|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|ijzer|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|isarn|īsarn|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Eisen|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|isarn|ísarn|lang=non}}, {{term|járn|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|jern|lang=da}} or {{term|jærn|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|järn|lang=se}}), Gothic {{term|𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂𐌽||tr=eisarn|lang=got}}, Old Irish {{term|īärn|lang=sga}} (Irish {{term|iarann|lang=ga}}, Welsh {{term|haearn|lang=cy}}). The Celtic source was related to Latin {{term|aes||bronze|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈiːzern/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|g=n|head=īsern|pl=[[isern|īsern]]}}

# the metal [[iron]]
# an instrument or tool made from iron, especially an iron [[sword]]
# an iron [[shackle]] or [[fetter]]

===Adjective===
'''īsern'''

# consisting or made of [[iron]]

